# `s/\(ab\)c/YES/` -- a group, which POSIX BRE has and this now implements.
#
# **This case has been in three states in one day.** It began as a silent
# defect: `\(` was a literal parenthesis, so this substituted the line holding
# the text `(ab)c` and left the line holding `abc` alone -- the inverse of the
# oracle on both counts, with no error and exit 0, while sed.sol's header
# claimed such a script "will be refused rather than misread".
#
# It then went to differ/ as a refusal, which was honest and still not sed.
#
# It is here now because lib/re.sol implements groups, and the answer is the
# oracle's. The three states are worth one comment: a case that moves from
# differ/ to agree/ is what closing a gap looks like from the corpus's side.
args: 's/\(ab\)c/YES/'
input:
xx (ab)c xx
yy abc yy
zz nothing zz
